(* ============================================================================
   16. FOOTNOTES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-025] (governs `footnote` / `footnote_definition`
      in PART 3). The reference form `[^label]` + `[^label]: body` and the
      INLINE form `^[content]` are implemented, and they are the only note
      forms: the proposed sidenote (`[>content]`) was dismissed as a
      presentation concern, so `[>` is unclaimed and `[>foo]` is literal
      text (docs/dismissed-syntax.md). Numbering and resolution are formalized
      as PART 9R rule R2; the rendering rules below stay normative here.
      - A `[^label]` reference with a matching definition is NUMBERED by
        document reference order (first referenced = 1); the same label
        referenced again reuses its number.
      - Definitions may appear anywhere (order-independent); the FIRST
        definition for a label wins. A body is the def line plus any
        following lines indented to column 2 or more (PART 9 §24 C1 column
        arithmetic -- a tab reaching column 2 qualifies the same as two
        spaces; single blank lines allowed between chunks), parsed as
        blocks. A definition is
        document-level metadata: it is COLLECTED and RESOLVED even when it
        sits INSIDE A CONTAINER. The reference resolves to the one endnotes
        section and the container that held the definition renders as
        though the line were never written -- §17 L6 states the general
        rule once, for every block-level container that can hold a
        definition, rather than enumerating them per definition kind. The
        corpus pins this (resources/examples/edge-cases.md "Footnote definition inside a
        container is collected").
      - A reference with NO matching definition renders as literal source
        text `[^label]` (a trailing `{attrs}` on an unresolved ref is not
        round-tripped -- a marginal mistyped-label edge). An UNREFERENCED
        definition is dropped.
      - Rendering (djot-compatible roles):
          reference -> <a id="{refId}" href="#fn{n}" role="doc-noteref"><sup>{n}</sup></a>
          endnotes  -> ONE <section role="doc-endnotes"> appended AFTER
                       all body content (outside heading <section>s),
                       containing <hr> then an <ol> of <li id="fn{n}">;
                       each note's last paragraph gets a trailing backlink
                       <a href="#{refId}" role="doc-backlink"
                          aria-label="{name}">↩</a>.
        First reference to note n has refId `fnref{n}`; a k-th (k>1)
        repeat uses `fnref{n}-{k}` and adds another backlink. The
        backlink glyph is the plain return arrow `↩` (Carve's choice;
        djot appends a variation selector).
        THE BACKLINK CARRIES AN ACCESSIBLE NAME -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-026]. The `role` alone
        was right and the NAME was the glyph, so a screen reader announced the
        character's Unicode name or skipped the link outright. `{name}` is the
        `footnoteBacklink` label (PART 9 §16a) followed by WHAT THE LINK VISIBLY
        SAYS -- the label alone for a lone backlink (`↩`, so
        `aria-label="Back to reference"`), the label plus k for the k-th of
        several (`↩<sup>k</sup>`, so `aria-label="Back to reference 2"`).
        Matching the visible text is WCAG 2.5.3, and it is why the number here
        is the REFERENCE ORDINAL and not the note number. The name is TEXT and
        is attribute-escaped.
        THE ENDNOTES SECTION CARRIES AN ACCESSIBLE NAME -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-027]. The rule
        above names the LINKS and left the REGION they sit in anonymous. The
        section takes `aria-label` when it has no authored title. The string is
        the `endnotes` label (SS16a), escaped the same way:
            <section role="doc-endnotes" aria-label="Footnotes">
        A placed `::: footnotes` with a quoted title instead takes
        `aria-labelledby` at that title's id under §12 [CARVE-P9-072].
        The default is "Footnotes" rather than "Endnotes" because that is the
        word the rest of this grammar uses for the construct. A host that
        renders them at the end and calls them endnotes sets the label; that is
        what the map is for.
      - A TOP-LEVEL `::: footnotes` THAT PLACES THE ENDNOTES SECTION -- NORMATIVE
        [CARVE-P9-075]. When at least one note is referenced, the first
        top-level `::: footnotes` places the one endnotes section at its
        position. Render each block authored in that directive's `children`
        in source order immediately BEFORE the section, as sibling blocks in
        the surrounding document or heading section. The authored blocks are
        not children of the generated endnotes section. The opener's quoted
        title and `[label]`, when present, instead become the section's first
        children under PART 9 §12 [CARVE-P9-072], before its `<hr>` and `<ol>`.
        Later `::: footnotes` markers, and a marker in a document with no
        referenced notes, render the ordinary `<div class="footnotes">` floor
        of §12 with their own title, label, and authored children. A nested
        marker follows [CARVE-P9-073] below, even when it appears before the
        first top-level marker.
      - DOCUMENT-WIDE PLACEMENT MARKERS REQUIRE DOCUMENT TOP LEVEL -- NORMATIVE
        [CARVE-P9-073]. A `::: footnotes`, `::: bibliography`, or
        `::: references` marker inside a block-level container -- a block
        quote, a list item, a div or directive body, a table cell, a definition
        description, a footnote definition -- does not place its document-wide
        region. It renders the `<div class="{kind}">` floor of §12 where it is
        written, and the region goes where it would without THIS marker. A
        processor with lint diagnostics reports `{kind}-placement-in-container`
        for the kind it supports. This rule does not restrict `::: toc`,
        `::: glossary`, or `::: index` inside a container.
      - A MARKER IN A TOC BODY RETAINS ITS CONTAINER SCOPE -- NORMATIVE
        [CARVE-P9-076]. With the `toc` placement extension enabled, a
        document-wide placement marker inside its body remains subject to
        CARVE-P9-073. Render its div fallback before the generated `<nav>`,
        where the extension emits its authored body. Moving that body ahead
        of the nav does not promote the nested marker to document top level.
        Its region goes where it would without that marker, including the
        default position when there is no other eligible placement marker.
      - BACKLINK PLACEMENT WHEN THE BODY DOES NOT END IN A PARAGRAPH. "The
        note's last paragraph" above presumes there is one. When the note
        body's LAST BLOCK is not a paragraph (a code block, a block quote, a
        table, a div, a raw block, or any other non-paragraph block), the
        backlink is never appended into a paragraph found INSIDE or BEFORE
        that block - the engine SYNTHESIZES a new wrapping <p> immediately
        AFTER the last block and places the backlink there, as a sibling of
        that block, never a descendant of it. This holds even when the last
        block itself ends in a paragraph of its own (a block quote whose
        content ends in a paragraph): that paragraph belongs to the quoted
        (or otherwise contained) content, not to the note's own backlink, so
        appending there would misattribute the backlink to the quotation. A
        note body consisting ONLY of a non-paragraph block still gets the
        synthesized trailing <p>. Appending to whatever paragraph happens to
        be last is undefined when the last block has no paragraph.
      - INLINE FOOTNOTE `^[content]` (pandoc form; a deliberate carve
        extension -- canonical djot has no inline footnotes). A `^`
        IMMEDIATELY before `[` opens an anonymous note whose content is the
        balanced bracket span (escape- and code-span-aware close, same as link
        text). Properties:
          * Content is INLINE-only, parsed recursively with footnote
            recognition DISABLED inside it (no `^[…]` or `[^ref]` nested in a
            note, either direction).
          * Numbered in the SAME document-order sequence as reference notes
            (an inline note always takes a fresh anonymous number; it cannot
            be re-referenced). Renders the same noteref + an `<li id="fn{n}">`
            in the one endnotes section, content as one `<p>` + backlink.
          * A trailing `{attrs}` attaches to the noteref `<a>` (like a
            reference note).
          * Empty or whitespace-only (`^[]`, `^[ ]`) is literal; an unclosed
            `^[…` is literal.
        PRECEDENCE (PART 8): `^[` opens an inline note; a bare `^` anywhere
        else is literal text (there is no bare superscript). Consequences:
        `^[x]^` is a note then a literal `^`; `\^[x]` is literal; a `^` not
        immediately followed by `[` is plain text.
      - LIMITATION: a footnote (reference `[^1]` or inline `^[…]`) inside link
        text (`[t[^1]](u)`) or inside a heading later cloned by a `</#id>`
        crossref nests an <a> in an <a>; avoid footnotes in those positions.

   16a. ENGINE-WRITTEN LABELS are defined in the full PART 9 §16a clause below.

   17. TIGHT vs LOOSE LISTS + CONTINUATION MARKER -- OPERATIONAL (governs
      `list` rendering and the continuation_marker productions in PART 2).
      L1 LOOSE(list) := some item is followed by a blank line before the
         next sibling marker, OR some item holds a blank-line-separated
         second PARAGRAPH; otherwise TIGHT. This is a statement about ONE
         and TWO blank lines: a run of THREE OR MORE is SS11 N1a's hard
         boundary, so the marker below it is not a "next sibling marker" at
         all and there is no one list left to be loose. Tightness is a property of the
         WHOLE ITEM, not of an individual block: a tight item's paragraphs
         ALL render WITHOUT `<p>` (<li>text</li>), every one of them, not
         only the first; a loose item's paragraphs are ALL wrapped
         (<li><p>text</p></li>). An item can hold more than one paragraph
         block and still be tight -- L2 and L6 below cover the ways that
         happens without a blank line ever appearing.
      L1a THE ITEM'S FIRST BLOCK DOES NOT MATTER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-029]. L1 asks
         whether the item holds a blank-line-separated second PARAGRAPH. It
         does not ask what the FIRST block was, and nothing here depends on
         that. So

             - - a

               Body.

         is LOOSE: the sub-list is the item's first block, `Body.` is a
         second paragraph after a blank line, and L1 is satisfied. The same
         holds for every other lead -- plain text, a heading, an attribute
         block, a block quote -- all of which core rendering handles
         loose.

      L1b AN INVISIBLE LINE DOES NOT CANCEL THE SEPARATION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-030]. L1
         asks whether the item holds a blank-line-separated second PARAGRAPH.
         An INVISIBLE LINE (§10 I5, AN INVISIBLE LINE IS ONE CLASSIFICATION)
         emits no visible block, which is why it cannot BE the second one
         (L1a's argument), and by the same clause's property 3 it
         does not choose the next line's owner either: it is not a separator,
         so the separation it appears to interrupt is intact and the item is
         LOOSE. This clause is that property's consequence for list
         TIGHTNESS.

             - a

               %% n
               text

         is loose. `text` is a second paragraph, the blank line precedes it,
         and the comment renders nothing.

      L1c THE BLANK IS READ AT THE LIST'S LEVEL, NOT THE ITEM'S. L1's first
         disjunct asks what stands BETWEEN one item and the next sibling
         marker. A blank line with nothing of the item after it stands between
         them however the item's interior accounted for the line -- including
         where an unterminated container carries it as content and the output
         shows it doing so, as an unterminated code fence's empty payload line
         does. So

             - ```           - ::: d
               b               b

             - s             - s

         are both LOOSE, and so are the same documents with the closer
         written. The state of the container above the blank does not enter
         this question either, which is the same property PART 1 S4 already
         states for where the item ENDS.

         An INTERIOR fence blank does not loosen the list: content follows it
         before the sibling marker, so no blank line separates the items --

             - ```
               a

               b
               ```
             - c

      L2 COMPACT LIST BLOCKS -- Carve deviation from djot/CommonMark. A
         blank line before an item's sub-BLOCK (sub-list, block quote,
         fenced code, fenced div, heading, table) does NOT loosen: the item
         stays tight, lead text inline, block attached. Only a genuine
         second PARAGRAPH (blank + indented plain prose), or a blank line
         BETWEEN items, loosens. The blank line is still required to START
         the block, so recognition and the uniformity principle are
         unchanged -- only the tight/loose RENDERING differs (djot renders
         these loose). Pinned by corpus compact-list-blocks.
      L3 CONTINUATION MARKER `+` -- Carve addition. A line whose only
         content is `+`, at the current container's MARKER COLUMN, attaches
         the FOLLOWING flush-left block to that container -- ONE block of
         ANY kind (paragraph, list, fenced code, table, block quote, div,
         ...), up to the next blank line, sibling marker, or a further `+`
         -- with no marker prefix or indentation, keeping the container
         tight. `+` is not a Carve bullet (§11 N1), so a lone `+` is
         unambiguous; outside any container a lone `+` is literal text.
         The marker only ATTACHES, never separates: a blank line is the
         sole way to END a container, so a `+` can never break the
         following block OUT of its container -- it adds it IN.

         CONTINUATION-MARKER FLUSH-LEFT MEANS COLUMN 0 -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-031].
         "flush-left" above is the reach, not a description of the usual case:
         the marker attaches a block that begins at column 0 and NOTHING else.
         A line at any other column is not attached at all - it falls through
         to the ordinary column rules, which place it by its own column inside
         whichever container survives. A `+` at a column no container's marker
         column names was never this marker: like the lone `+` outside any
         container above, it is ordinary text and reaches the output.

         The single-level spelling is unaffected in practice, which is why this
         went unnoticed: under `- +` a line at column 2 is the item's OWN
         content column, so the ordinary rules put it in the item whether the
         marker reached it or not.

         ONE IS A COUNT AND THE BOUNDARY IS NOT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-032].
         The blank line, sibling marker or further `+` above is the EXTENT of
         that single block, which may be many lines long -- a list, a quote,
         a fenced block. It is not a run boundary, and the marker does not
         attach everything up to it. So

             - a
             +
             para
             > q

         attaches `para` and leaves the quote OUTSIDE the item, and a second
         attached block takes a second marker:

             - a
             +
             para
             +
             > q

         THE MARKER IS ONE OPERATION IN EVERY CONTAINER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-033].
         The clause above is the WHOLE definition of `+`:
         ownership of the next flush-left block passes to the container, and
         that block is then parsed like any other. A container may not answer
         differently because of WHAT it is attaching, and there is no second
         rule anywhere for a quote, a list, a fence, a caption or a definition
         under the marker. In particular the ONE BLOCK count and the boundary
         set above hold in EVERY container in L4, not only in the two that had
         them spelled out.

      L4 CONTAINERS THAT TAKE THE MARKER:
         - LIST ITEM (pinned by corpus list-continuation-marker): `+` at
           the item's marker column attaches the block to the item (for
           code/tables one would rather not indent). FIRST-BLOCK form: the
           marker may sit on the marker line itself (`- +`, a bare `+`) to
           open an item whose body is the following flush-left BLOCK, one
           of them as everywhere else in L3, with no inline lead;
           `- + text` keeps `+ text` as literal text.
         - BLOCK QUOTE: `+` at column 0 immediately after a blockquote line
           attaches the following flush-left block to the quote -- the
           un-prefixed alternative to repeating `>`. "> quoted \n + \n
           - item" is a quote whose body is the paragraph "quoted" PLUS a
           real <ul> (not a lazily-folded paragraph, cf. §10 I6). A blank
           line after the quote still ends it. An attached block that is
           ITSELF a quote NESTS, like every other kind: `> a` / `+` / `> q`
           is a quote holding a paragraph and an inner quote. The marker
           attaching nothing there, and the line continuing the outer quote
           instead, was the kind test named in L3.
         - FOOTNOTE DEFINITION body: `+` at column 0 attaches the following
           flush-left block to the note, the alternative to indenting it to
           the body's column (pinned by corpus
           footnote-with-multiple-blocks).
         - DEFINITION DESCRIPTION: the same marker, the same reach, for a
           `<dd>` (`definition_body` carries `continuation_marker_block`).

         THE FIRST-BLOCK FORM IS THE ITEM AND THE DESCRIPTION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-067].
         The four containers above take the marker; only TWO of
         them take it in the FIRST-BLOCK position. `- +` and `: +` open a body
         whose content is the following flush-left block, and over a line that
         is not flush-left the marker is refused and the body ends. A FOOTNOTE
         BODY and a BLOCK QUOTE have no such form: `[^a]: +` and `> +` open a
         body whose first line is a lone `+`, and there the `+` is NOT A MARKER
         AT ALL. It is ordinary text, the payload lands wherever the ordinary
         column rules put it, and no column is read as a marker column --

             [^a]: +
             flush

         is a note whose text is `+` with `flush` a top-level paragraph, and

             > +
             flush

         is a quote holding one paragraph `+ flush`, the second line reaching
         the first by §10 I6's lazy fold. At the body's own column the payload
         folds into the paragraph the `+` opened, so `[^a]: +` over a column-2
         line is the single paragraph `+ flush` inside the note.

         That is a DIFFERENT ANSWER from "place the payload by its column", not
         a narrower one, which is why it is stated rather than left to the
         reader of L3. Extending the first-block form to the other two
         containers was the alternative, and it is not what this clause does.
      L5 NOT CONTINUATION CONTAINERS (a lone `+` keeps its plain meaning):
         - fenced containers (admonition / generic div / line block) --
           their bodies already hold flush-left blocks; a lone `+` inside
           is literal text;
         - TABLE -- a first-column `+` is the continuation ROW (§5 T6), a
           distinct construct;
         - LEAF blocks (heading, thematic break, code block, raw block,
           paragraph) hold no child blocks; a heading is a bounded title.
      L6 A COLLECTED DEFINITION LEAVES NO TRACE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-034] (the general
         rule for PART 9R R1/R2 COLLECTION, stated ONCE here for every
         container that can hold a definition). This is §10 I5's property 2 --
         emits no visible block at its authored location -- for the two
         definitions that also REGISTER, which is their semantic action; the
         property itself is stated at AN INVISIBLE LINE IS ONE CLASSIFICATION
         and is not re-derived here. A link reference or
         footnote definition is document-level metadata: wherever it is
         written, PASS 1 collects it into the shared symbol table and PASS
         2 resolves uses against it from anywhere in the document (PART
         9R). The BLOCK-LEVEL CONTEXT that held the definition line renders
         as though that line were never written at all -- not an empty
         paragraph standing in its place, not a blank line, not residual
         whitespace. This holds for EVERY block-level context that can hold
         block content: a LIST ITEM, a BLOCK QUOTE, a FOOTNOTE BODY (§16),
         a DEFINITION-LIST `dd`, and a block attached by a `+` CONTINUATION
         MARKER (L3/L4) alike. A future container that can hold block content
         is covered by the same rule.

         "No trace" is about OUTPUT, not about block structure -- it does
         not exempt a definition line from §10 I5, which already governs
         paragraph interruption everywhere, containers included (I6): a
         definition line INTERRUPTS an open paragraph exactly as any other
         invisible construct does, ending it with no blank line needed, so
         a plain line immediately after a definition starts a NEW block
         rather than folding back into the paragraph the definition
         interrupted. What decides how that new block RENDERS is L1/L2, not
         L6: no blank line appeared anywhere, so the item stays TIGHT
         (L2 loosens on a blank line, never on block count alone), and by
         L1 a tight item's paragraphs are ALL bare, this new one included --
         not because the definition is transparent to structure, but
         because nothing here ever loosened the item in the first place.
         A footnote definition sitting on a list item's OWN line, followed
         by a plain continuation line, is this exact shape: the item holds
         TWO paragraph blocks with no blank between them and renders both
         bare, `<li>first\n  second\n</li>`. Treating the definition as if it
         both interrupted AND merged would need a rule found nowhere else in
         this grammar; treating it as loosening the item on its own would
         contradict L2 directly.

      L7 ONE CONSUMED BOOLEAN SPELLS THE LOOSENESS NO BLANK LINE CAN --
         NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-035]. A container's preceding BLOCK-ATTRIBUTE LINE
         (§15) MAY carry the boolean `loose`. It says that the container's
         children render as BLOCKS rather than as inline runs, and it is
         CONSUMED: it never reaches the output as an HTML attribute. The
         precedent is PART 12 §15's `header-rows`, which rides the same line,
         carries a structural fact as a boolean, and is likewise consumed rather
         than emitted.

             {loose}
             - Note text.

         renders

             <ul>
               <li><p>Note text.</p></li>
             </ul>

         THE KEY IS NOT LIST-SPECIFIC, and writing it as if it were would have
         been the first of at least two one-off keys. The tight/loose axis
         exists in exactly TWO places, and this key applies wherever it exists:
         a LIST (L1) and a DEFINITION LIST (PART 2, definition lists), and
         any container a later clause gives the axis to, with no second key to
         invent. A table cell and a caption suppress the paragraph wrapper with
         no wrapped alternative at all; a block quote, div, line block and
         footnote body always wrap. On a container with no such axis the name
         has no special meaning and renders `loose=""` like any other boolean.

         WHAT IS UNSPELLABLE WITHOUT IT. Looseness is spelled with a blank line,
         and a blank line needs two things to stand between, so the shape with
         nothing on one side has no spelling:

           - a ONE-ITEM list. `<ul><li><p>x</p></li></ul>` has no "between
             items" to put a blank line in.
           - a definition description holding ONE block. A blank line between
             two ENTRIES does not loosen a `<dl>` at all -- only a second block
             inside the description wraps it -- so `<dd><p>x</p></dd>` is
             unspellable at every entry count, not just at one.


         THERE IS NO `tight` TWIN, and its absence is a decision rather than an
         omission. TIGHT IS ALWAYS SPELLABLE: remove the blank lines. Only loose
         has an unspellable case, so a `tight` key would be surface with no
         shape behind it -- and it would introduce a genuinely ambiguous
         document, where the blank lines say loose and the attribute says tight.

         THE NAME IS THE TERM OF ART, on purpose. A name describing the tree
         would name the wrong fact -- the children are already block nodes in
         both spellings, and what moves is how they RENDER -- and a name
         describing the output would be an HTML artifact, where this axis has to
         mean something on the Markdown, plain and terminal targets too. `loose`
         is what CommonMark, djot and Markdown Extra all call this distinction.

         REDUNDANT USE IS A NO-OP. `loose` on a list the blank lines already
         made loose is legal and changes nothing, and so is `loose` on a
         description that already holds two blocks. Rejecting it would make the
         key context-sensitive, and a producer that always emits it is simpler
         than one that has to decide.

         THE CANONICAL WRITER EMITS IT ONLY WHERE THE BLANK-LINE SPELLING
         CANNOT. This follows PART 12 §15, whose writer "retains `header-rows` /
         `footer-rows` when they are present" rather than deriving them onto
         every table, and PART 11 §2, which spends a mark only where omitting it
         would change the re-parsed document. On a multi-item loose list the
         blank lines already say it, so the key would be an idle mark.

         THE TEST IS A RE-PARSE, AND IT IS OVER THE DOCUMENT. Write the
         container's body without the key, read it back, and emit the key
         exactly where the container's own looseness field did not survive that
         trip. The equality is PART 11 §1's, taken over the re-parsed document;
         taking it over the RENDER instead answers a different question.

         THE ITEM COUNT IS NOT THE TEST. A ONE-ITEM list is loose whenever the
         blank line sits INSIDE that item, and there the blank-line spelling did
         express the looseness, so a writer keyed on the item count rewrites
         such a document and fails `parse(fmt(x)) == parse(x)`. The count is
         sound as a SHORTCUT IN ONE DIRECTION ONLY: two or more items always
         re-parse loose, so the read may be skipped there.

         THE COUNT IS ALSO WRONG IN THE OTHER DIRECTION. A definition list's
         entries count as two or more on any list worth writing, and a blank
         line between two ENTRIES does not loosen a `<dl>` at any count - so a
         writer keyed on the count OMITS the key from the container that needs
         it most.

         ON A DEFINITION LIST THE ANSWER IS UNCONDITIONAL: the writer emits the
         key on every definition list carrying `loose`. PART 12 §8 publishes
         `definition_list.loose` ONLY where the looseness was SPELLED, so a body
         written without the key can never read back with the field set, and the
         re-parse test says "emit" every time. A description that already holds
         two blocks does not change it: there the key is redundant in the RENDER
         and not in the TREE, and the tree is what the equality is taken over.

         ORDERED AND NESTED CONTAINERS TAKE THE SAME KEY AT THE SAME PLACEMENT,
         at the nested container's own indent, so this needs no rediscovering:
         `{loose}` before `1.` loosens an `<ol>`, and an attribute line written
         at a sub-list's indentation loosens the sub-list and not its parent.

         A BOOLEAN AND AN EMPTY VALUE ARE ONE KEY; A VALUED ONE IS NOT. PART 4
         makes `{loose}` and `{loose=""}` the same attribute, so both are
         consumed. `loose=x` names a value this key does not take, so it is not
         this key: it stays an ordinary attribute and renders `loose="x"`. There
         is no error state to define, and no spelling that half-applies.

         EACH CONTAINER CARRIES THE KEY'S EFFECT IN ITS OWN FIELD, and PART 12
         §6's round trip covers both. A loosened LIST sets `list.tight` false --
         the field is required there, and the axis is total, so nothing else is
         needed. A loosened DEFINITION LIST sets `definition_list.loose`, which
         PART 12 §8 defines: the `<dd>` wrapper is otherwise derived from the
         description's block count, and that derivation cannot reach the one
         shape this key exists for, so the SPELLED fact is published and nothing
         else is. The two fields are named differently because they
         say different things: `tight` states the whole axis for a list, while
         `loose` records only what a definition list's own derivation misses.


   18. MATH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-036] (governs `math` in PART 3; djot form). Inline
      `$` + verbatim backtick span; display `$$` + verbatim backtick
      span. A `$` NOT immediately followed by a backtick run is literal
      (`$5` is currency); `\$` escapes a literal `$`. Rendering:
        inline  -> <span class="math inline" role="math">\(content\)</span>
        display -> <span class="math display" role="math">\[content\]</span>
      `content` is the verbatim span text, HTML-escaped (`&`,`<`,`>`).
      Display math alone on a line renders inside its own paragraph. A
      standalone display-math block carrying a trailing caption (PART 9 §4)
      is a numbered EQUATION: the math paragraph is wrapped in a figure and a
      `</#id>` to it resolves to "Equation N" (§19).
      Author `{attrs}` after the span merge onto the <span> (the base
      `math …` class is kept; see PART 10 §1).

      A directly numbered display equation renders its label and number after
      the math span as one U+0020 followed by
      `<span class="equation-number">LABEL NUMBER</span>`, with one U+0020
      between LABEL and NUMBER and no brackets. `attrs.id` remains on the math
      span, so a resolved cross-reference lands on the formula. Plain, ANSI and
      Markdown append one U+0020 followed by the same `LABEL NUMBER` text, with
      no brackets, under their ordinary inline escaping rules.

      A MATH SPAN CARRIES ROLE MATH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-037]. The delimiters are written
      for a typesetter to find. Until one runs -- and if none ever does, which
      is the default -- the span is a plain `<span>` full of backslashes and
      carets, and a reader announces them as prose. `role="math"` says the run
      IS MATHEMATICS whether or not the script arrives.

      THE NAME STAYS THE AUTHOR'S, and no key is added to §16a for it. A
      formula's spoken form is per-formula content, not an engine word, and the
      seam for it already exists: `{attrs}` merge onto this same span, so a
      trailing {aria-label="E equals m c squared"} needs nothing new. An author
      who spells their own `role` keeps it -- under any ASCII casing, for the
      reason SS12 states.

      `role` is written LAST, after the merged author attributes, so adding it
      never moves an attribute the author placed (the ordering PART 10 §1
      pins for the base class).

      AN IMPORTER WRITES THE CORE FORM, NEVER THE EXTENSION FENCE --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-038]. An importer meeting `<div class="math display">`
      emits the CORE display form -- `$$` followed by a verbatim backtick span,
      a paragraph holding a `math` node -- and never the ```` ```math ````
      fence. `<span class="math inline">` is the inline `$` form on the same
      terms.

      THE REASON IS THAT AN IMPORTER MUST NOT EMIT A CONSTRUCT WHOSE MEANING
      DEPENDS ON THE CONSUMER'S CONFIGURATION. The ```` ```math ```` fence is
      an EXTENSION. With that extension not loaded it degrades to a
      `language-math` code block, so the same imported document is mathematics
      for one consumer and a code block for another. `math_display` is core
      (PART 3) and needs nothing loaded.

      THE ROUND-TRIP ARGUMENT IS REAL AND LOSES ANYWAY. A math-block extension
      WRITES that div, so the fence is an exact round trip of the document that
      produced the HTML. But an importer's job is to produce a document that
      means what the HTML meant, and it cannot know that an extension generated
      it at all.

      The importer MUST NOT choose the fence based on whether an extension is
      registered; identical input produces the same core form.

      THE MATHML PATH WRITES NO TRAILING DELIMITER, for the reason the core
      form has none to write. Carve math is a PREFIX on a code span, so the
      span's own closing backtick ends the construct: a `<math>` read through
      its `alttext` is written `` $`E = mc^2` `` inline and ``` $$`E = mc^2` ```
      for `display="block"`, with no `$` or `$$` after the closing backtick. A
      trailing delimiter is literal text, and it renders as a stray dollar sign
      beside the formula.

   19. CROSSREF AUTO-TEXT + DEFAULT-ON / PROCESSOR FEATURES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-039]
      (resolution formalized as PART 9R rules R4/R5; behavior detail here).
      - `</#id>` clones the target heading's full inline children, so a
        heading with markup (`# *Setup*`) yields a link that KEEPS the
        markup (`<a href="#…"><strong>Setup</strong> …</a>`).
      - A `</#id>` cross-reference resolves to exactly ONE LEVEL: it links
        to the target and adopts the target's text, FLATTENING any nested
        cross-reference in that text (a `</#…>` in the cloned heading text is
        not re-expanded). One-level resolution makes a self-reference
        (`# A </#a>`) and a mutual cycle (`# A </#b>` + `# B </#a>`) SAFE --
        each side resolves once, no infinite expansion. A normal, non-cyclic
        crossref still resolves. The corpus pins this (resources/examples/edge-cases.md
        "Cyclic cross-reference resolves to one level").
      - `</#id>` to a NUMBERED CAPTION (a figure / table / listing / equation
        whose caption carries a number placeholder and an `{#id}`) yields the
        caption's LABEL + NUMBER ("Figure 1"), markup preserved, NOT the
        caption prose. A directly numbered display equation resolves through
        its `attrs.id` and yields its string LABEL + NUMBER ("Equation 1"). A
        `</#id>` to an id that is neither a heading nor either numbered target
        stays unresolved and renders literally.
      - Mentions (`@user`), tags (`#tag`) and smart typography are ON by
        default in the conformant core; a processor MAY disable them. The
        corpus pins the default-on behavior.
      - SOCIAL LINK RESOLUTION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-070]. Mention and tag
        syntax is core; turning either node into a link is opt-in processor
        configuration. A host MAY supply a MENTION resolver, a TAG resolver,
        or both. For each parsed node of the configured kind, the processor
        gives the resolver the node's kind (`mention` or `tag`), its exact
        parsed name, its AST attributes, and an opaque read-only host context.
        The resolver returns ONE complete link destination or UNRESOLVED. An
        implementation MAY expose this as a synchronous or asynchronous API;
        the observable result is the same.

        THE RESULT CHANGES ONLY THE DESTINATION. A resolved mention or tag is
        rendered through the same link form as a configured URL template. Its
        visible label remains the authored `@name` or `#name`; its structural
        `mention` / `tag` class and AST attributes remain governed by PART 3
        and PART 10. A resolver cannot return HTML, replacement inline content,
        classes, attributes, or another Carve node. Its result is never parsed
        as Carve and never starts another resolution pass.

        RESOLVER PRECEDENCE IS PER KIND. When a resolver is configured for a
        kind, it is authoritative for that kind: a returned UNRESOLVED value or
        an error renders the ordinary inert `<span>` and MUST NOT fall through
        to that kind's URL template. A processor SHOULD report a resolver error
        as a diagnostic, but MUST NOT fail the whole render solely because one
        social token did not resolve. When no resolver is configured for the
        kind, the existing `mentionUrl` / `tagUrl` template behavior applies.
        When neither mechanism is configured, the node remains an inert span.

        A TEMPLATE receives a percent-encoded name before substitution. A
        resolver receives the exact parsed name and returns a complete
        destination, so the processor MUST NOT encode or interpolate that
        result a second time. Both template and resolver destinations MUST pass
        the PART 9 section 25 URL-scheme denylist; a denied destination is
        UNRESOLVED and renders the inert span. The callback is host code, not a
        permission to perform network or database access: a host that does so
        owns authentication, authorization, timeouts, request bounds, and
        tenant containment. The processor invokes at most one configured
        social resolver per parsed node and MUST NOT rescan a returned value.
      - FILE INCLUSION / TRANSCLUSION (`{{ … }}`) is a PROCESSOR-LEVEL
        directive, NOT part of the core parser. Its syntax is PART 6; its
        normative semantics are below and mirrored, with worked examples, in
        docs/includes.md. A conformant CORE never recognizes the directive
        (unreachable from `block`/`inline`) and leaves `{{ … }}` LITERAL --
        it parses the bytes under ordinary inline rules, so a bare-path
        directive with no active inline markers emits verbatim (corpus
        "Include directive with no resolver renders literal"). The rules a
        conforming PROCESSOR that DOES expand includes MUST follow:

        I1 SYNTAX. `{{ path }}`, `{{ path #section }}`, `{{ path @key:value }}`,
           combinable `{{ path #section @key:value }}` (PART 6). `path` is bare
           (stops at space / `#` / `@` / `}`) or double-quoted for spaces, and
           is resolved RELATIVE TO THE INCLUDING FILE. Options split into two
           disjoint kinds. SELECTION options choose WHICH content is pulled in:
           `#section` (semantic -- the subtree rooted at the heading whose id
           equals `section`, that heading through content up to the next
           same-or-higher-level heading) and `@lines:N-M` (physical -- a 1-based
           inclusive line range). The two SELECTION mechanisms are MUTUALLY
           EXCLUSIVE: a directive MUST select by `#section` OR by a line-range,
           NEVER both; a directive carrying both is an ERROR (Warning + literal,
           per I7). TRANSFORM options reshape already-selected content and are
           orthogonal to selection: `@shift:N` shifts every included heading's
           level by the SIGNED integer N (I8). `@shift` MAY accompany `#section`,
           a line-range, or neither. `@shift` takes EITHER a signed integer OR
           the literal value `auto` (context-relative, computed by the processor
           per I8), never both. Every other `@key` is RESERVED.

        I2 BLOCK vs INLINE. A directive ALONE on its own line is a BLOCK
           include: the resolved content merges as BLOCKS at the directive
           site. A directive within inline content is an INLINE include: the
           resolved content MUST parse (as an I4 fragment) to INLINE-ONLY
           content -- a single paragraph, or nothing -- and its inlines are
           spliced into the surrounding inline sequence. Resolved content
           with any other block structure in inline position is an ERROR
           (Warning + literal, per I7). Mirrors the §10 block/inline model.

        I3 RESOLUTION MODEL. The CORE performs NO file I/O. The HOST supplies a
           resolver with the contract: given (path, section?, options?,
           includingFileContext) it returns resolved Carve source text OR an
           error. If NO resolver is configured the directive is left LITERAL
           (the processor emits the verbatim `{{ … }}`). This mirrors the
           bibliography loader precedent (host resolves, parser stays pure;
           docs/extensions.md §6.1) and preserves browser / WASM parity.
           OPT-IN: no resolver is configured by default, and a host MUST leave
           it off for untrusted input unless it has satisfied PART 9 §25 (File
           inclusion).

        I4 MECHANISM (normative on OUTCOME, permissive on mechanism). The
           observable result MUST be AS IF the resolved content appeared inline
           at the directive site, SUBJECT TO FRAGMENT CONTAINMENT: included
           content is parsed as a SELF-CONTAINED FRAGMENT. A construct still
           open at the end of the included content CLOSES at child EOF (an
           unclosed fence or div terminates there, exactly as at the end of a
           standalone document); included content NEVER captures or
           reinterprets parent content that follows the directive. An
           implementation MAY splice source pre-parse OR parse-and-merge
           events / AST -- but ONLY where the result equals the fragment-
           containment outcome. The two diverge exactly when the child ends
           inside an unterminated construct (a naive textual splice would let
           an unclosed child fence swallow the rest of the parent); that
           result is NON-conformant. This matters doubly for `@lines:N-M`,
           whose physical slice can cut a fence or div in half: the torn
           construct is bounded to the fragment, never the parent. Source
           positions SHOULD be remapped so source-mapped hosts can attribute
           included spans to the child file (Carve's AST already distinguishes
           snippet-local from document-space offsets; carve-js src/ast.ts
           `Position`).

        I5 CROSS-FILE COLLISIONS. An identifier that expansion brings together
           is handled according to whether it is DOCUMENT-VISIBLE or FILE-LOCAL.

           DOCUMENT-VISIBLE IDENTIFIERS -- DETERMINISTIC RENAME-ON-COLLISION.
           EXPLICIT heading ids (the link targets of `</#id>` cross-references)
           and FOOTNOTE labels (footnotes are collected and numbered globally in
           the assembled document) are document-visible: they are exposed by the
           assembled document and can be targeted across file boundaries. When
           expansion brings together duplicates of either, the FIRST occurrence
           in document order keeps its label / id and each later duplicate is
           renamed. Order is: parent before child, and an earlier include before
           a later include (the fully expanded document read top to bottom).
           The rename appends the least `-N` (integer N >= 2) that is not already
           taken in that namespace: first `-2`, then `-3`, and so on. Explicit
           heading ids and footnote labels are SEPARATE namespaces. References to
           a renamed target follow the rename, so the renamed target's own
           `</#id>` cross-references and footnote references still resolve to it.
           A Warning MUST be emitted per rename.

           FILE-LOCAL IDENTIFIERS -- SCOPED, NOT RENAMED. A REFERENCE-DEFINITION
           label is file-local metadata: it is resolved WITHIN the document that
           defines it, BEFORE that document is merged into its parent. Two files
           MAY therefore use the same label with different destinations, and each
           file's references resolve to its own definition -- a parent `[a][]`
           resolves to the parent's `[a]: /PARENT` and a child `[a][]` to the
           child's `[a]: /CHILD`. This is NOT a collision: it MUST NOT emit a
           Warning and MUST NOT rename anything.

           RATIONALE: a reference-definition label is never addressable from
           another file, so renaming it would be pure churn plus a spurious
           warning. An explicit heading id or a footnote label genuinely IS
           document-visible, so a duplicate there must be resolved and surfaced.

           SCOPE: the include-time rename pass covers EXPLICIT heading ids and
           footnote labels ONLY. Auto-generated (slug) heading-id collisions are
           NOT part of it; they continue to dedup via the existing heading-id
           tracker (§13, which suffixes duplicate slugs once the files are
           merged). ORDERING: the include-time explicit-id / footnote rename runs
           BEFORE the §13 slug dedup, so ids are deterministic (the explicit-id
           namespace is settled first, then §13 dedups auto-slug ids against the
           final set).

        I6 LIMITS. The processor MUST detect inclusion CYCLES (a file that
           transitively includes itself): the offending directive is left
           literal, not expanded, and a Warning is emitted. Cycle detection is
           the PRIMARY guard. As a SECONDARY DoS bound, implementations MUST
           enforce a FINITE include-depth limit; the RECOMMENDED default is at
           least 16 (a small, human-scale nesting bound, far under the structural
           MAX_NESTING = 200 of §25) and the host MAY configure it. No exact hard
           constant is pinned. A directive at greater depth is left literal with
           a Warning. Total expanded output is charged against the same per-
           render byte budget as other amplifying features -- `max(1 MB,
           8 x input length)` (§25); once exceeded, further expansion degrades
           to the literal directive with a Warning. The byte budget bounds the
           expanded OUTPUT; it does NOT bound the WORK done to produce it,
           because a target is resolved before its size is known and a document
           may carry one directive per dozen bytes. Implementations MUST
           therefore enforce a FINITE bound on RESOLVER INVOCATIONS per render;
           the RECOMMENDED default is at least 1000, and the host MAY configure
           it. A directive past the bound is left literal with a Warning and
           MUST NOT be passed to the resolver. The byte budget and the
           resolver-call bound are whole-render TOTALS that only ever grow, so
           once either is exhausted, later directives MUST be refused BEFORE
           invoking their resolver -- not resolved and then refused -- and the
           two bounds therefore limit reads as well as emitted output.

        I7 ERRORS. Unreadable path, binary / non-text content, BOTH selection
           mechanisms present (`#section` + a line-range, I1), block-structured
           content in INLINE position (I2), cycle, depth-exceeded,
           size-exceeded, call-bound-exceeded, and a directive reached AFTER
           byte-budget or call-bound exhaustion EACH emit a Warning and leave
           the directive LITERAL (visible and debuggable). The last two are
           never passed to the resolver at all (I6). Inclusion NEVER silently
           drops a directive. See PART 9 §25 (File inclusion) for the security
           model: parser stays pure, resolver is host-supplied and MUST enforce
           canonical path containment after symlink resolution and MUST NOT
           fetch remote URLs without an explicit allowlist. Included content
           receives the same sanitization as its parent. The processor-neutral
           vectors in `tests/include-security-conformance/` pin these security
           requirements separately from the broader include-language suite.

        I8 HEADING-LEVEL SHIFT (`@shift:N` | `@shift:auto`). A TRANSFORM option
           (I1), orthogonal to selection. `@shift` takes EITHER a SIGNED integer
           OR the literal value `auto`, NEVER both.
           LITERAL FORM. N is a SIGNED integer; every heading in the included
           content has its level increased by N (N=2: an h1 becomes an h3; a
           NEGATIVE N raises levels, N=-1: an h2 becomes an h1). An omitted
           `@shift` is `@shift:0` (no shift, the default; current behavior
           unchanged). CLAMP: the resulting level is clamped to the valid range
           [1, 6]; a shift that would push a heading below 1 or above 6 clamps to
           the bound and emits a Warning -- the heading is KEPT, never dropped.
           IDS/SLUGS UNCHANGED: `@shift` changes only the level, never a
           heading's id / slug (ids are name-based), so `</#id>` cross-references
           into shifted headings still resolve. AUTO-NUMBERING: if section
           auto-numbering (HeadingNumbers, docs/extensions.md) is enabled,
           shifted headings renumber at their NEW level as a consequence.

           AUTO FORM (`@shift:auto`). CONTEXT-RELATIVE: the processor COMPUTES
           the shift instead of taking it from the author.
           CONTEXT LEVEL C: the level of the nearest PRECEDING heading, in
           document order, that is in the directive's OWN block container or an
           ENCLOSING (ancestor) container. A heading inside a SIBLING container
           that has already CLOSED does NOT set the context. If no such heading
           exists, C = 0.
           TOP LEVEL T: the MINIMUM heading level present in the resolved
           content, AFTER any `#section` or line-range selection has been
           applied. The MINIMUM (not the first heading's level) is used so the
           included document's internal relative structure is preserved.
           COMPUTED SHIFT: N = (C + 1) - T. N is then applied EXACTLY as a
           literal shift under the rules above -- clamp to [1, 6] with a Warning
           on clamp, heading KEPT never dropped, ids / slugs unchanged so
           cross-references still resolve, and auto-numbering renumbering at the
           new level.
           NO HEADINGS: if the resolved content contains NO headings, `auto` is a
           NO-OP (N = 0) and emits NO Warning.
           NESTED INCLUDES: `auto` resolves against the document AS ASSEMBLED at
           that point, so a shift already applied by an ANCESTOR include is in
           effect when a nested `auto` is computed. Expansion proceeds
           OUTSIDE-IN, which makes the result DETERMINISTIC.
           INLINE INCLUDES: inline content cannot contain headings, so `auto` on
           an INLINE include (I2) is a no-op.
           EXAMPLE: an including document with `## Chapters` before the directive
           gives C = 2; a child starting at `# Chapter One` gives T = 1; so
           N = (2 + 1) - 1 = 2 and the child's h1 renders as an h3.
           RATIONALE: `auto` makes a chapter file PORTABLE -- the same file can
           be included at any depth without editing its heading levels. Because
           the author writes `@shift:auto` explicitly at the include site, the
           position-dependence is OPT-IN PER DIRECTIVE, not ambient behavior.

        I9a RECOGNITION SHAPE -- RUN, NOT NODE. Grammar-level recognition does
           NOT mean "one parsed text node matches the directive grammar". A
           directive's own syntax overlaps constructs the core already parses:
           `#section` is TAG syntax, `@key:value` is MENTION syntax, and a
           double-quoted path is rewritten by smart quotes, so by the time the
           include pass runs `{{ x #intro @shift:1 }}` is SEVERAL adjacent nodes.
           A processor MUST recognize a directive over a CONTIGUOUS RUN of inline
           nodes carrying only LITERAL TEXT content (text, tag, mention, and any
           other node whose source form is recoverable verbatim): reassemble the
           single nodes silently drops every directive carrying `#section` or an
           option, and every directive appearing mid-sentence. The run STOPS at
           any node that is not literal-text-shaped, so a directive interrupted
           by emphasis, a link, or a code span is NOT recognized and stays
           literal (same rule as a bare-path directive with active inline
           markers).

        I9 VERBATIM PROTECTION. An include directive is recognized ONLY where
           inline and block constructs are recognized. Inside a code span, a
           fenced or raw code block, or any other verbatim context, `{{ ... }}`
           is ordinary LITERAL text and is NEVER resolved. This is a consequence
           of Carve's general invariant that CODE IS VERBATIM -- no construct
           transforms inside a code span or code block -- of which the include
           directive is no exception. A literal `{{ ... }}` can therefore always
           be written and displayed by placing it in code.

        I10 CONTAINMENT ROOT + CANONICAL CONTAINMENT CHECK. Every resolved path
           is checked against a single CONTAINMENT ROOT.
           DEFAULT ROOT (RECOMMENDED): for FILE-BASED entry points (a CLI
           invocation on a document path, or a convert-from-file API) the root
           SHOULD default to the DIRECTORY OF THE TOP-LEVEL DOCUMENT -- a
           document path is already known, so requiring the host to name a root
           explicitly is not necessary. The root MUST NOT default to the process
           working directory (arbitrary with respect to the document, and
           possibly `/` or a home directory).
           NO PATH CONTEXT, NO DEFAULT: for STRING-INPUT APIs there is no
           document path and therefore no inferable root; a host MUST supply the
           root explicitly, otherwise inclusion stays DISABLED and directives
           remain LITERAL (I3). This preserves the opt-in posture for embedders.
           A CONFIGURED ROOT MUST BE ABSOLUTE: any other root spec is REFUSED,
           leaving inclusion DISABLED and directives LITERAL as with no root.
           The test is absoluteness, not emptiness: a whitespace-only spec is
           refused as relative, and an absolute path to a directory named with
           spaces is a valid root.
           ONE ROOT PER EXPANSION: relative include paths resolve relative to the
           INCLUDING file (I1), but containment is checked against the single
           TOP-LEVEL root. The root MUST NOT re-base per included file; a
           re-based root shrinks at every nesting level, so a nested document
           could never reference a sibling directory of the project.
           CANONICAL, NOT LEXICAL (MUST): a processor MUST canonicalize the
           candidate path (resolving symbolic links) and then verify the
           canonical result is contained within the canonical root. A `..`
           segment is PERMITTED exactly when the canonical result stays inside
           the root. A lexical `..` rejection is wrong on both sides: TOO STRICT
           (it rejects legitimate sibling-directory layouts, e.g. a document in
           `chapters/` including `../shared/glossary.crv` whose target is inside
           the project root) and TOO WEAK (symbolic links and absolute paths
           escape a root with no `..` present at all). Absolute paths remain
           DENIED unless they canonicalize inside the root. Given the layout
           `book/{main.crv, chapters/ch1.crv, shared/glossary.crv}` with root
           `book/`: from `chapters/ch1.crv`, `../shared/glossary.crv` is ALLOWED
           and `../../../etc/passwd` is DENIED. See PART 9 §25 (File inclusion)
           and docs/security.md.
           A containment refusal MUST NOT reveal whether the target exists.
           For a missing path, canonicalize the longest existing prefix and
           normalize the remaining path before checking containment. Present
           and absent targets outside the root MUST both be refused as
           containment denials, without reading or checking the existence of
           the target itself first.

        I11 DEPENDENCY REPORTING. A processor that expands includes MUST report,
           alongside the expanded document and any Warnings, the SET OF INCLUDE
           TARGETS it touched during the expansion.
           SCOPE: the set covers the ENTIRE recursive expansion, including
           nested children, DE-DUPLICATED, in a DETERMINISTIC order.
           IDENTITY: each target is identified by the resolver's CANONICAL ID
           where one is supplied -- the same identity the cycle guard uses in
           I6 -- and otherwise by the RESOLVED PATH. Two spellings that name
           the same document MUST return the same id (`a/b.crv`, `a/./b.crv`).
           An unresolved target's id is resolved against the including file
           like a resolved one, so it names where the file would appear; a
           path that escapes the containment root (I10) keeps the directive's
           spelling. So does a target that exists but the resolver refuses.
           So does a request carrying a URI scheme, which names no place.
           ATTEMPTED TARGETS ARE INCLUDED: the set MUST contain targets that
           were ATTEMPTED but NOT resolved -- missing or unreadable, binary,
           containment-denied (I10), cycle-broken (I6), and depth- or
           size-exceeded (I6). RATIONALE: a host that watches only the files it
           successfully read never learns that a target it could not find has
           since been CREATED, so a preview would stay stale at exactly the
           moment the author fixes the problem.
           DISTINGUISHABILITY: each entry MUST be distinguishable as RESOLVED
           versus ATTEMPTED-BUT-UNRESOLVED.
           PURPOSE: hosts use this set for INVALIDATION (file watching) and for
           DIAGNOSTICS. It is a CROSS-IMPLEMENTATION CONTRACT, so an editor can
           rely on it regardless of which engine backs it. Without it, preview
           invalidation cannot be implemented correctly. See docs/includes.md
           (Reported dependencies, and Editor and IDE integration).

        I12 THE WRITER PRESERVES A DIRECTIVE VERBATIM. A Carve writer MUST NOT
           escape the braces of a well-formed directive; it writes the directive
           as spelled, including one whose shape is well-formed but whose option
           is unknown or whose `#section` is not a valid identifier. Inside a
           verbatim context `{{ … }}` is not a directive (I9).

        I15 A PROCESSOR TARGETING CARVE SOURCE DOES NOT EXPAND. When the output
           target is Carve source (`fmt`, a `--carve` render, any pipeline whose
           product is Carve), the expansion pass MUST NOT run, whether or not a
           resolver is configured. Other targets expand as usual.

   20. RAW PASSTHROUGH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-040] (governs `raw_inline` in PART 3 and the
      semantics of `raw_block` in PART 2). The BLOCK form (```=FORMAT
      fence, PART 2) emits its verbatim content UNESCAPED when FORMAT
      matches the output format and drops it otherwise; the inline form
      follows. Block and inline raw share the `=FORMAT` spelling (```=html
      / `…`{=html}). A code span whose trailing attribute block is EXACTLY
      `{=format}` (a single `=`-prefixed format name, with no other
      classes/ids/keys) is raw passthrough: the verbatim span content
      is emitted UNESCAPED when `format` matches the output format (`html`),
      and DROPPED otherwise. The `{=format}` tag is consumed, not rendered.
      A code span with any OTHER trailing `{…}` is a generic attributed code
      span (PART 3 `code_span` note), not raw inline. The corpus pins this
      (tests/corpus/50-raw-inline):
        `<br>`{=html}   -> <br>            (emitted verbatim)
        `\foo`{=latex}  ->                (dropped in HTML output)

   ============================================================================ *)
